The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one fix. The reports in #4471 show more
than one problem. Each problem has its own cause and needs its own fix. The fix for the parser
leaves the other problems in place.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one known issue. Each problem below has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {repo} {issues} />

| problem | what the reporter sees | cause |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | Day and month swap: 7 April arrives as 4 July. | The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant in #4471 writes the day first. |
| `hint` | Day and month swap, but only for dates typed into the booking form. | The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type month first, and the form reads the date day first. |
| `one-day` | The date moves by one day: Tuesday 12 May arrives as Monday 11 May. | A day and month swap cannot explain a move of one day. The reports point at time zones: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." |

A tenant cause places each report: where the tenant is, and which form it runs (`causes.ts`). The
intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, so this page ignores it. The desk read some
reports as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). Those reports form the `one-day` problem.

## How the desk merged them

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The desk merged reports with different subject lines into #4471. Decision 0001 merges only on an
exact subject match, so the merge did not follow the desk's own rule. A subject line never shows
the cause: the same subject "Booking on the wrong date" comes from the `parser` problem and from
the `hint` problem.

## For the desk

- Count each report once. The ticket export repeats some reports, and `uniqueById` removes the copies.
- Before you merge a report into a big ticket, check its tenant, its channel, and its dates.
- A date that moves by one day is not a day and month swap. Keep it apart.

